<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ho-Chunk</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Winnebago</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ho-Chunk, also called the Winnebago, are a Siouan-speaking Native American people of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, whose homeland lay in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>. Some of their warriors joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s confederacy and fought at Tippecanoe in 1811. Pressed by treaties and removals in the 19th century, they are today divided between the Ho-Chunk Nation of Wisconsin and the Winnebago Tribe of Nebraska.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chunk.jpg" alt="Ho-Chunk" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winnebago_Indan_Congress_Members.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ho-Chunk, also known as Hoocąk, Hoocągra or Winnebago, speak a Siouan language that they believe their creator, Mą’ųna, gave them. Their name, meaning People of the Sacred Voice, comes from an oral history in which they are the originators of the Siouan languages. They say that their ancestors built the thousands of effigy mounds of the Late Woodland period, and they claim descent from both the mound builders and the Oneota culture that followed. In the 16th century they were the dominant people of their territory, living in permanent villages and growing corn, squash, beans and wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, and hunting and fishing. Winnebago is the name used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a>, adopted by the French and English.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The French explorer Jean Nicolet made contact in 1634 and found the Ho-Chunk around Green Bay. Their population fell sharply in the 17th century, perhaps to about 500, a loss that has been attributed to the drowning of several hundred warriors in a storm on a lake, to epidemics of European diseases and to war with the Illinois Confederacy, and to competition from Algonquian peoples who had moved west. It recovered to more than 2,900 by 1806, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic of 1836 killed nearly a quarter of the people. They ceded land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> in 1829, 1832 and 1837.</p><p>Through the 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> moved them to reservations in Iowa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a>, South Dakota and finally Nebraska; oral history holds that some of them were forcibly moved as many as thirteen times. After the Dakota War of 1862, about 2,000 Ho-Chunk were interned at Camp Porter in Mankato and expelled in 1863 to Crow Creek, in Dakota Territory. Many left for Nebraska, where the Winnebago Reservation was founded in 1865, and many returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, often resisting removal without violence by going back home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Ho-Chunk traditionally had twelve clans, each with its own role: the Thunderbird Clan supplied chiefs and the Bear Clan kept discipline. People generally married outside their clan. Women grew and processed food and gathered roots, nuts and maple sap, and men hunted and, when needed, fought. Boys fasted at puberty to gain a guardian spirit.</p><p>Two separate sovereign nations are federally recognised today: the Ho-Chunk Nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, with its headquarters at Black River Falls, and the Winnebago Tribe of Nebraska, whose reservation lies in Thurston County. About 12,000 people belong to the two. In 2015 it took control of a 1,500-acre parcel in Sauk County, Wisconsin, the site of the former Badger Army Ammunition Plant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ho-Chunk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Half-Moon way was introduced in 1914 or 1924 by James Seymour, who was a member of the <b>Winnebago</b> tribe.</blockquote><a href="/religion/native-american-church">Native American Church · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…religion has to be reconstructed from objects, from the layout of towns and burials, and from comparison with the beliefs of descendant peoples recorded since the 18th century, such as the Muscogee, Cherokee, Natchez, Osage and <b>Ho-Chunk</b>; these comparisons are valuable but uncertain, since centuries separate the two.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederacy united warriors from the Shawnee, Kickapoo, Potawatomi, Wyandot, <b>Ho-Chunk</b> and others under Tecumseh's leadership and his brother's religious authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/tecumsehs-confederacy">Tecumseh's Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
